{
  "version": 3,
  "description": "Canonical CHAT symbol registry. `symbols` is the single owner of what each named symbol MEANS and how it parses; the ca_* character arrays consumed by the grammar and the model are DERIVED from it. `character_classes` are sets of characters with no individual identity, which is a different kind of thing and stays a set.",
  "field_notes": {
    "id": "The symbol name, and the ONLY spelling of it. The Rust variant is PascalCase of this and the serde tag is this verbatim, both derived; storing either beside it would be the same mirror this registry exists to remove.",
    "codepoint": "The identity. The character is derived from it, never stored, because homoglyphs are invisible in a diff and a codepoint is not.",
    "parse_role": "What the GRAMMAR does with it. word_attached joins the word token; paired_stretch opens and closes a region.",
    "notation_family": "WHERE it comes from. Independent of parse_role: two of these are disfluency marks from the CHAT manual Disfluency Transcription chapter, not Conversation Analysis notation, and CLAN names them NOTCA_CROSSED_EQUAL and NOTCA_LEFT_ARROW_CIRCLE. Never gate CA leniency on a ca_* array; gate it on this field.",
    "example": "A CHAT fragment that MUST parse. Generated into a parser test, so an example that stops working fails the build."
  },
